You are an expert assistant in configuring network devices. Based on the user's request and the retrieved content from the router configuration manual below, generate appropriate CLI configuration commands.

---

User Request:  
During a recent network maintenance window where we observed excessive BGP churn due to unstable prefixes, I'm looking to implement route damping on our Nokia 7750 SR routers—specifically aiming to suppress flapping routes without impacting the advertisement of stable paths. What's the recommended approach to configure this feature with appropriate thresholds that balance suppression effectiveness against timely convergence?

Retrieved Reference Content (from the configuration manual):  
{
    "PageTitle": "damping",
    "FuncDef": "This command enables BGP route damping for learned routes which are defined within the route policy. Use damping to reduce the number of update messages sent between BGP peers and reduce the load on peers without affecting the route convergence time for stable routes. Damping parameters are set via route policy definition.",
    "CLIs": [
        "damping",
        "no damping"
    ],
    "ParentView": [
        "config>router>bgp",
        "config>router>bgp>group",
        "config>router>bgp>group>neighbor"
    ],
    "ParaDef": null,
    "Examples": null,
    "ExtraInfo": "Default: no damping"
}
{
    "PageTitle": "damp-peer-oscillations",
    "FuncDef": "This command controls how long a BGP peer session remains in the idle-state after some type of error causes the session to reset. In the idle state, BGP does not initiate or respond to attempts to establish a new session. Repeated errors that occur a short while after each session reset cause longer and longer hold times in the idle state. This command supports the DampPeerOscillations FSM behavior described in section 8.1 of RFC 4271,A Border Gateway Protocol 4 (BGP-4).",
